# Fixed for this nil-only call (lib/sake/typer_eval.rb): when every path that goes on is one no value takes (the
# raise ends the nil path, the other is narrowed to nothing), the rest of the function is not run. This prints
# "a" and the message. Sample: test/samples/raise_if_nil_then_rest.strict.sake.
# NOT the case of controller_view's _form partial, whose workaround stays: there post is nil | Post in one call,
# so form_with's model is narrowed to Post after the raise, but the block reads the caller's post, which is the
# same value under another name, and stays nil | Post (narrowing it would need the checker to follow that the
# argument and the captured variable alias, or to split a call per type of a union argument: a redesign).
# After `raise ... if model == nil` with a model that is only nil, the rest of the function is still checked, here
# the block, which reads the caller's `post` (nil in that call): "Post.title: argument 1 must be Post, but is nil".
# Ruby: form(nil) raises before the yield; this prints "a" and the message. Not caused by 04cea37b (the same with
# fbfd2733). Seen in test/sakelib/rails/actionpack/controller_view.sake once the checker got past the construction:
# ActionView's form_with_options raises on a nil model, but the _form partial's block (`Post.errors_count(post)`) was
# checked with the post of a nil @post. Workaround in the Sake template: `if post && Post.errors_count(post) > 0`.
class Post
  attr_reader title
end
def form_with(model)
  raise ArgumentError, "Passed nil to the :model argument" if model == nil
  yield
end
def form(post) = form_with(post) { Post.title(post) }
p(form(Post.new("a")))
begin
  form(nil)
rescue ArgumentError => e
  puts(Exception.message(e))
end
